# ============================================================
# Response Format Standards
# ============================================================

REST resource naming, versioning, and HTTP semantics: **`09-api-design.mdc`**. This rule defines the JSON response envelope only.

---

# Philosophy

Every API response must follow
one consistent contract.

Clients should never need
endpoint-specific parsing logic.

Consistency is more important
than convenience.

---

# Standard Response Envelope

Every HTTP response must follow

```json
{
    "data": {},
    "meta": {},
    "error": null
}
```

Exactly three top-level fields exist.

- data
- meta
- error

Never add additional root fields.

---

# Successful Responses

Successful responses always have

```json
{
    "data": ...,
    "meta": {},
    "error": null
}
```

Error must always be null.

---

# Error Responses

Error responses always have

```json
{
    "data": null,
    "meta": {},
    "error": {
        "code": "",
        "message": "",
        "details": {}
    }
}
```

Never return partial success objects.

Error JSON MUST be produced by **global exception handlers** in `app/core/exception_handlers.py`, not ad hoc in routers.

See `12-errors.mdc` for mapping and logging rules.

---

# Root Fields

Root field meanings

data

Contains requested resource.

meta

Contains metadata.

error

Contains failure information.

---

# Root Field Rules

Every response MUST contain

data

meta

error

Never omit any field.

---

# Response DTOs

All responses must use
dedicated Response DTOs.

Never return

ORM Models

Domain Entities

database rows

raw dictionaries

---

# DTO Naming

Examples

UserResponse

SchoolResponse

ReportResponse

RoleResponse

TeacherResponse

PaginatedUserResponse

---

# Serialization

Routers are responsible for

serializing DTOs.

Use Cases return

Domain Entities

or

Application DTOs.

Repositories never serialize.

---

# Null Rules

Success

```json
"error": null
```

Failure

```json
"data": null
```

Never mix both.

---

# Collections

Collections must always return

```json
[]
```

Never return

null

for lists.

---

# Empty Results

No matching resources

↓

```json
{
    "data": [],
    "meta": {
        "total": 0
    },
    "error": null
}
```

Avoid inventing custom responses.

---

# Single Resource

Return one object.

Example

```json
{
    "data": {
        "id": 1
    },
    "meta": {},
    "error": null
}
```

---

# Create Responses

POST

↓

201 Created

Example

```json
{
    "data": {
        "id": 100
    },
    "meta": {},
    "error": null
}
```

---

# Delete Responses

DELETE

↓

204 No Content

or

200

using the standard envelope.

Choose one project-wide convention.

Be consistent.

---

# Update Responses

PATCH

PUT

should return

the updated resource

whenever practical.

---

# Metadata

Meta contains

pagination

execution metadata

warnings

timing

request identifiers

Never place business data
inside meta.

---

# Pagination Metadata

Standard pagination

```json
{
    "meta": {
        "offset": 0,
        "limit": 20,
        "total": 350,
        "has_next": true
    }
}
```

---

# Optional Metadata

`request_id`/`correlation_id` are not optional — see "Request ID and
Correlation ID" below; they are auto-filled on every response.

Meta may additionally contain

timestamp

execution_time_ms

api_version

when appropriate.

---

# Request ID and Correlation ID

Every HTTP response should include tracing ids in `meta`, auto-filled from
`structlog.contextvars` via `tracing_meta()` in `app/core/tracing.py` (called
by `app/shared/responses/envelope.py`) when `meta` is not explicitly passed.
`tracing_meta()` lives in `app/core/` rather than `app/shared/responses/`
because it is application-wide cross-cutting infrastructure, not a response-
shape concern — `01-folder-structure.mdc` reserves `shared/` for genuinely
generic code and `core/` for exactly this kind of thing (alongside
`logging.py`, `middleware.py`):

- `correlation_id` — primary support reference; survives across services
- `request_id` — identifies this API hop only

The same values appear in response headers (`X-Correlation-ID`,
`X-Request-ID`) and on every log line for the request.

---

# Timestamp

Prefer ISO-8601 UTC.

Example

```text
2026-07-28T10:15:00Z
```

---

# Execution Time

Execution time

is infrastructure metadata.

Never expose internal profiling.

---

# Resource Representation

Responses describe

business concepts

not database tables.

---

# Sensitive Data

Never expose

passwords

password hashes

tokens

refresh tokens

JWT secrets

API secrets

internal identifiers

database implementation details

SQL queries

stack traces

---

# Internal Fields

Never expose

created_by_internal

deleted_flag

row_version

internal_status

unless required by API contract.

---

# Enum Serialization

Serialize enums

using stable API values.

Never expose

Python enum internals.

---

# Boolean Values

Return

true

false

Never use

0

1

"true"

"false"

---

# Numbers

Return numeric types.

Do not serialize numbers

as strings

unless precision requires it.

---

# Dates

Use ISO-8601.

Always include timezone

when applicable.

---

# Null Values

Return null

only when

business meaning exists.

Avoid excessive nulls.

---

# Field Naming

All API fields

must use

snake_case.

Never mix

camelCase

PascalCase

snake_case.

---

# DTO Stability

DTOs define

the public contract.

Database changes

must not automatically

change DTOs.

---

# Versioning

Breaking DTO changes

require

new API versions.

---

# Nested Objects

Return nested resources

only when explicitly required.

Avoid deeply nested responses.

---

# Response Size

Only include

necessary fields.

Avoid over-fetching.

---

# Success Messages

Avoid

```json
{
    "message": "Success"
}
```

Business data belongs

inside data.

---

# Human Messages

Error messages

belong inside

error.message

Never place messages

inside data.

---

# Error Codes

Every error must contain

stable machine-readable codes.

Example

```json
{
    "code": "USER_NOT_FOUND"
}
```

---

# Error Details

details contains

field validation

constraint violations

context

Never expose

stack traces.

---

# Warnings

Warnings belong

inside meta.

Example

```json
{
    "meta": {
        "warnings": [
            "Using deprecated field."
        ]
    }
}
```

---

# Hypermedia

Do not implement HATEOAS.

Keep responses simple.

---

# File Downloads

Binary downloads

should stream files.

Do not wrap binary content

inside JSON.

---

# Streaming Responses

Streaming endpoints

may bypass

the standard envelope.

Document exceptions.

---

# Health Endpoints

Health endpoints

may return simplified payloads.

Document exceptions.

---

# Authentication Responses

Authentication endpoints

still follow

the standard response envelope.

Tokens belong

inside data.

---

# Consistency

Identical operations

must produce

identical response structures.

---

# Documentation

Every response model

must appear

in OpenAPI documentation.

---

# Testing

Every endpoint should verify

response shape

status code

DTO serialization

metadata

error envelope

---

# Cursor and Claude MUST NEVER

Generate raw dictionaries

Generate ORM Models

Generate Domain Entities

Generate SQLAlchemy Models

Generate inconsistent root fields

Generate missing meta

Generate missing error

Generate missing data

Generate stack traces

Generate passwords

Generate internal database fields

Generate camelCase

Generate inconsistent pagination

Generate different error structures

Generate stringified booleans

Generate stringified numbers

Generate business messages outside data

Generate HTTP-specific fields inside DTOs

Generate transport-specific logic inside Domain

---

# Canonical Success Example

{
    "data": {
        "id": 15,
        "name": "John Doe",
        "status": "ACTIVE"
    },
    "meta": {
        "correlation_id": "8b7b34ab-...",
        "request_id": "3f2c9a10-..."
    },
    "error": null
}

---

# Canonical Error Example

{
    "data": null,
    "meta": {
        "correlation_id": "8b7b34ab-...",
        "request_id": "3f2c9a10-..."
    },
    "error": {
        "code": "USER_NOT_FOUND",
        "message": "The requested user does not exist.",
        "details": {}
    }
}

`correlation_id`/`request_id` are always present (auto-filled — see
"Request ID and Correlation ID"). `api_version` is not currently
implemented; add it here once a router actually sets it.

---

# Final Principle

Every response is a contract.

Clients should be able to parse every endpoint using the same response envelope, regardless of the resource, business operation, or HTTP status code.

A response should reveal business information while hiding implementation details.